The key invariant is that a raised `OVERFLW` places the FSM in `b` or `f`. The next two transitions cannot raise it again, including when reset occurs.

**Verification prerequisite:** As written, `prop` has no sampling clock and is not asserted. Add `@(posedge clk)` to `prop`, and assert it and the lemmas. A property declaration alone performs no check. The registers also require an initial reset for this proof; without one, an unconstrained initial state can violate `prop`. ([fpga.mit.edu](https://fpga.mit.edu/6205/_static/F25/documentation/1800-2017.pdf))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) (OVERFLW == 1'b1) |-> ((stato == b) || (stato == f)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Only the e branch raises OVERFLW, and that branch moves the FSM to b or f. This ties an overflow to the phase from which the two-cycle proof begins."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) ((stato == b) || (stato == f)) |=> ((OVERFLW == 1'b0) && ((stato == c) || (stato == g) || (stato == a))); endproperty",
      "explanation": "From b or f, the next state is c or g and overflow clears; reset instead moves to a and also clears overflow."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clk) ((stato == a) || (stato == c) || (stato == g)) |=> (OVERFLW == 1'b0); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Transitions from a, c, and g never raise overflow, even if reset occurs. Together with the first two lemmas, this establishes OVERFLW == 0 two clocks after any asserted OVERFLW."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

`OVERFLW` is set only when state `e` executes. That transition moves the FSM to `b` or `f`; the next transition moves it to `c` or `g`, which clear overflow. The lemmas expose those intermediate states so the two-cycle obligation can be proved step by step.

The supplied `prop` has no clocking event, so check it at `posedge clk` (for example, `assert property (@(posedge clk) prop);`). The proof also needs a reset-before-checking constraint: without reset, the registers are unconstrained and the property can fail. ([fpga.mit.edu](https://fpga.mit.edu/6205/_static/F25/documentation/1800-2017.pdf))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (OVERFLW |-> ((stato == b) || (stato == f))); endproperty",
      "explanation": "After reset, only state e sets OVERFLW, and it simultaneously transitions to b or f. This links an asserted overflow to the FSM phase needed for the proof."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) ((!rst && ((stato == b) || (stato == f))) |=> (((stato == c) || (stato == g)) && !OVERFLW)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "With reset inactive, b and f both transition to c or g and clear overflow. This establishes the first step after an overflow observation."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clk) (((stato == c) || (stato == g)) |=> !OVERFLW); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Both c and g clear overflow on their next transition; reset clears it as well. Combined with the preceding lemmas, this establishes the target's two-cycle consequent."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

Assume `prop` is asserted on `posedge clk` and reset is applied at startup. The property itself specifies no sampling clock; `|-> ##2` checks the value two sampled cycles later. ([accellera.org](https://www.accellera.org/images/eda/sv-ec/1507.html?utm_source=openai)) Without a startup reset constraint, arbitrary initial register values can falsify the property.

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) (OVERFLW == 1) |-> ((stato == b) || (stato == f)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Only state e sets OVERFLW to 1, and it simultaneously moves to b or f. This ties an overflow pulse to its resulting FSM state."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) ((stato == b) || (stato == f)) |=> ((stato == a) || (stato == c) || (stato == g)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "From b or f, the next state is c or g; a synchronous reset instead selects a. This bounds the state one cycle after an overflow."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clk) ((stato == a) || (stato == c) || (stato == g)) |=> (OVERFLW == 0); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Each listed state, as well as reset, writes zero to OVERFLW. Combined with the first two lemmas, this establishes zero overflow two cycles after a pulse."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

The key invariant is that an asserted `OVERFLW` places the machine in `b` or `f`; neither state can produce another overflow within two clocks. The lemmas below assume `prop` is asserted on `posedge clk` and reset is applied before checking. As written, the module provides neither a clock for `prop` nor an assertion of it. ([rfsoc.mit.edu](https://rfsoc.mit.edu/6S965/_static/F24/documentation/1800-2017.pdf?utm_source=openai))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) disable iff (rst) (OVERFLW |-> (stato == b || stato == f)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Only state e sets OVERFLW, and that transition enters b or f. This inductive invariant ties the overflow flag to the correct pipeline phase."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) (!rst && (stato == b || stato == f)) |=> ((stato == c || stato == g) && !OVERFLW); endproperty",
      "explanation": "With no reset, b and f advance to c or g and clear OVERFLW. This establishes the first clock after an overflow."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clk) (!rst && (stato == c || stato == g)) |=> ((stato == wf0 || stato == wf1) && !OVERFLW); endproperty",
      "explanation": "With no reset, c and g advance to wf0 or wf1 while keeping OVERFLW clear. Together with the preceding lemmas, this establishes the target's second clock; a reset also clears the flag."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

The two-cycle delay is an FSM-phase question: `OVERFLW` is set only while processing `e`, which moves to `b` or `f`. Those states then move to `c` or `g`, neither of which sets overflow. The lemmas below expose those steps to the formal engine.

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) (OVERFLW == 1) |-> ((stato == b) || (stato == f)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Only state e sets OVERFLW, and that same transition moves stato to b or f. This links an observed overflow to its subsequent FSM phase."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) ((stato == b) || (stato == f)) |=> ((stato == a) || (stato == c) || (stato == g)); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Processing b or f moves to c or g; a reset instead moves to a. Thus the cycle following an observed overflow cannot process e."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clk) ((stato == a) || (stato == c) || (stato == g)) |=> (OVERFLW == 0); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Each listed state clears OVERFLW, as does reset. Together with the first two lemmas, this establishes OVERFLW == 0 two sampled cycles after an overflow."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

**Verification prerequisite:** `prop` needs a clock when asserted (or an inherited default clock). The invariant in `lemma_1` also requires reset-established initial state; with arbitrary unreset register values, the target property itself can fail.
